/**
 * @page coding_style Coding Style
 *
 * **← Back** @ref git_workflow "Git Workflow"
 * | **Next →** @ref docs_style "Documentation Style"
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Quick Links
 *
 * | | |
 * |-|-|
 * | @ref index "Developer Wiki" | @ref arch_overview "Architecture Overview" |
 * | @ref docs_style "Documentation Style" | @ref security_principles "Security Principles" |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Overview
 *
 * PicoKernel follows a consistent style across all layers. The goal is not
 * aesthetic uniformity for its own sake, it is readability, auditability, and
 * predictability. Code that looks the same everywhere is easier to review,
 * easier to debug, and easier to hand off.
 *
 * Style violations are flagged at review. Persistent deviations block merge.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Naming Conventions
 *
 * ### Functions
 *
 * All functions are prefixed with their layer initial and a descriptive verb.
 *
 * | Layer | Prefix | Example |
 * |-------|--------|---------|
 * | Kernel | `k_` | `k_alloc()`, `k_free()`, `k_panic()` |
 * | Drivers | `d_` | `d_uart_write()`, `d_spi_transfer()` |
 * | Modules | `m_` | `m_wifi_scan()`, `m_nfc_init()` |
 * | Interfaces | `i_` | `i_shell_run()`, `i_web_handle()` |
 *
 * Internal (non-public) functions use a double-underscore prefix after the
 * layer prefix:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // Public kernel API
 * void *k_alloc(size_t size);
 *
 * // Internal kernel helper, not exposed in headers
 * static void k__coalesce_block(heap_block_t *block);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ### Types and Structs
 *
 * Types use `snake_case` with a `_t` suffix. Struct tags are only used
 * when the struct contains a pointer to itself (e.g. linked list nodes).
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // Standard struct: anonymous tag, typedef only
 * typedef struct {
 *     size_t   size;
 *     uint8_t *data;
 *     uint32_t canary;
 * } heap_block_t;
 *
 * // Self-referential struct: tag required for forward reference
 * typedef struct block_header_t {
 *     size_t              size;
 *     struct block_header_t *next;
 *     struct block_header_t *prev;
 * } block_header_t;
 * @endcode
 *
 * ### Constants and Macros
 *
 * All constants and function-like macros use `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * #define HEAP_CANARY      0xDEADFACEDEADFACEULL
 * #define MAX_TASKS        16
 * #define ALIGN_UP(x, a)   (((x) + (a) - 1) & ~((a) - 1))
 * @endcode
 *
 * ### Variables
 *
 * Local variables and function parameters: `snake_case`, descriptive.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * size_t block_size = 0;
 * uint8_t *write_ptr = NULL;
 * @endcode
 *
 * Global variables: prefix with layer initial and double underscore:
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // Kernel-global scheduler state
 * static task_t k__task_table[MAX_TASKS];
 * static uint32_t k__task_count = 0;
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## File Organisation
 *
 * ### Directory structure
 *
 * @code
 * src/
 *   kernel/       - Allocator, scheduler, IPC, panic, init
 *   drivers/      - UART, SPI, I2C, GPIO, timer, flash
 *   modules/      - NFC, WiFi, packet capture, user modules
 *   interfaces/   - Serial shell, web terminal
 * include/
 *   version.h     - Single authoritative version definition
 * @endcode
 *
 * ### Header Guards
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * #ifndef PICOKERNEL_KERNEL_MEMORY_H
 * #define PICOKERNEL_KERNEL_MEMORY_H
 *
 * // ... declarations ...
 *
 * #endif /* PICOKERNEL_KERNEL_MEMORY_H */
 * @endcode
 *
 * Format: `PICOKERNEL_<LAYER>_<FILENAME>_H` in `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`.
 *
 * ### Recommended include order
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // 1. Corresponding header (for .c files)
 * #include "kernel/alloc.h"
 *
 * // 2. Other PicoKernel headers (alphabetical)
 * #include "kernel/panic.h"
 * #include "kernel/version.h"
 *
 * // 3. Pico SDK and any other lib/ headers (should be in < >)
 * #include <pico/stdlib.h>
 * #include <hardware/flash.h>
 *
 * // 4. C standard library
 * #include <stdint.h>
 * #include <stddef.h>
 * #include <string.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Formatting
 *
 * | Rule | Detail |
 * |------|--------|
 * | Indentation | 4 spaces - no tabs |
 * | Line length | 100 characters soft limit, string literals are exempt |
 * | Braces | K&R style - opening brace on the same line, except for function definitions |
 * | Pointer alignment | `uint8_t *ptr` - star with the variable, not the type |
 * | Blank lines | One between logical sections, two before a function definition |
 *
* @code{.c}
 * void *k_alloc(size_t size)
 * {
 *     if (size == 0) {
 *         k_panic("k_alloc: zero-size allocation requested");
 *     }
 *
 *     block_header_t *block = k__find_free_block(size);
 *
 *     if (block == NULL) {
 *         k_panic("k_alloc: out of memory");
 *     }
 *
 *     block->canary = CANARY;
 *     return (void *)(block + 1);
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @note Debug output functions such as `kdump()` may use
 * `// clang-format off` and `// clang-format on` guards around
 * long diagnostic `printf` lines. This is the only permitted use
 * of format guards. Do not use them to bypass style rules in
 * non-debug code.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Error Handling
 *
 * | Situation | Required behaviour |
 * |-----------|-------------------|
 * | Kernel invariant violated | `k_panic()` with descriptive message |
 * | Invalid parameter to public API | `k_panic()` or return error code and document which |
 * | Allocation failure | `k_panic()` no silent NULL return without documentation |
 * | Driver error | Return error code, do not swallow silently |
 * | Unreachable code path | `k_panic("unreachable: <context>")` |
 *
 * There are no silent failures. Every error path results in either an explicit
 * return code (documented in the function's `@return`) or a panic.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## C Language Rules
 *
 * | Rule | Rationale |
 * |------|-----------|
 * | C11–C23 (`-std=c11` minimum) | New code may use C23 features where the toolchain supports them |
 * | No implicit `int` | All functions and variables explicitly typed |
 * | No `goto` | Except for cleanup patterns in kernel init, document each use |
 * | `NULL` not `0` for pointers | Semantic clarity |
 * | `sizeof` not magic numbers for buffer sizes | Prevents silent truncation |
 * | Cast explicitly | Required for narrowing conversions and pointer casts, not general arithmetic |
 * | Unused parameters | Mark with `(void)param;` do not leave silently unused |
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // Correct
 * static void k__unused_cb(uint32_t event, void *ctx)
 * {
 *     (void)event;
 *     (void)ctx;
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Security-Relevant Coding Rules
 *
 * These overlap with @ref security_principles but are restated here for
 * completeness. All are enforced at review.
 *
 * | Rule | Detail |
 * |------|--------|
 * | Bounds-check before pointer arithmetic | Validate size before `ptr + offset` |
 * | No unbounded `memcpy` / `strcpy` | Always supply explicit sizes |
 * | Canary values on heap blocks | Never remove or bypass canary validation |
 * | Debug output behind `#ifdef KERNEL_DEBUG` | No diagnostic output in release builds |
 * | No direct hardware register access outside drivers | Architectural rule - review blocker |
 *
 * Full security policy: @ref security_principles.
 *
